# Terraform commands - cheat sheet

Run everything from **inside** an environment folder (`dev/` or `prod/`).
Check where you are first: `pwd`

## Every session

```bash
cd /etc/ansible/terraform/dev      # or prod
source ../env.sh                   # vCenter login (prompts for password)
```

## Core workflow

```bash
terraform init                     # first time in a folder, or after changing providers
terraform plan -out=tfplan         # preview changes and save the plan - READ IT
terraform apply tfplan             # do exactly what the saved plan says
```

## Day-to-day (edit terraform.tfvars, then plan + apply)

| Task | Change in terraform.tfvars |
| --- | --- |
| Add a VM | add a line |
| Resize CPU/RAM | change `size` |
| Bigger OS disk | `os_disk_gb = 40` (grow only, never shrink) |
| Extra data disk | `data_disk_gb = 50` |
| Other VLAN | `vlan = 1619`  |
| Other folder | `folder = "atm/Apps"` (must exist) |
| Other OS | `os = "centos"` (template must exist) |
| Decommission | delete the line |

## Look around

```bash
terraform output                   # VM names and IPs
terraform state list               # everything Terraform tracks in this folder
terraform state show 'vsphere_virtual_machine.vm["dev-ubuntu-01"]'   # one VM in detail
terraform show                     # everything, in full
```

## Check code without touching vCenter

```bash
terraform validate                 # check for mistakes
```

## Drift (someone changed a VM in vCenter)

```bash
terraform plan                     # differences show as ~
```
Then either `apply` to put it back, or update the code to match reality.

## Protected VMs (prod)

`prevent_destroy = true` in main.tf blocks any delete.
To decommission a protected VM:
1. comment out the `lifecycle` block
2. remove the VM's line from terraform.tfvars
3. `terraform plan -out=tfplan` - must show only that one destroy
4. `terraform apply tfplan`
5. put the `lifecycle` block back

## Bring existing objects under Terraform (import)

```bash
# an existing tag
terraform import vsphere_tag.prod '{"category_name": "Environment", "tag_name": "prod"}'
```

## Emergencies only

```bash
terraform plan -target='vsphere_virtual_machine.vm["dev-test-01"]'      # plan one VM
terraform destroy -target='vsphere_virtual_machine.vm["dev-test-01"]'   # destroy one VM
terraform destroy                                                       # destroy EVERYTHING in this folder
```
Normally remove the line from terraform.tfvars instead of using -target.